% ============================================================================
% separation-methods — the common laboratory methods for separating a mixture,
% each mapped to the property/basis it separates by (as its source states it)
% (adj-facts-stdlib, CHEMISTRY).
% ============================================================================
% A mixture is two or more substances physically combined, and because they are
% only PHYSICALLY combined they can be pulled apart again — but HOW you pull
% them apart depends on which physical property the components DIFFER in. That
% is the one big idea a chemistry agent reasons UP from: pick the separation
% method whose basis matches the property the two components do not share.
% "Filtration separates by particle size; distillation by volatility; chromato-
% graphy because the components move at different rates; evaporation drives the
% liquid off the solid." Before you can choose a method you must first know
% WHAT property it exploits. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? separation_basis(distillation, $Basis)   % by_volatility
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `separation_basis(method, basis)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% basis WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these separation methods (it never invents a basis).
%
% Each separation method and the property/basis its source states it separates
% by, as a little truth table:
%
%     method          separates by / what it separates      a beginner's cue
%     -------------   -----------------------------------   ---------------------
%     filtration      by_particle_size                      a porous filter traps big particles
%     distillation    by_volatility                         boil off the most volatile part first
%     evaporation     separates_liquid_from_solid           drive the liquid off, leave the salt
%     chromatography  by_different_rates                    parts travel through a medium at diff. speeds
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a basis and recall its method:
%
%     ? separation_basis($Method, by_particle_size)   % filtration
%
% A word that is NOT one of these separation methods — `centrifuge` is not in
% this source's list, `rock` is not a method at all — is deliberately NOT a
% row, so a separation recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a basis. That
% honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner
% on: it answers exactly the method→basis pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `basis` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% property/basis the source EXPLICITLY states for that method — never one the
% source does not support. Where the source's own word differs from the everyday
% word (it says "volatile"/"vaporizes", not "boiling point"; it says the
% components "move at different rates", not "adsorption"), the atom uses the
% source's own word, so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted
% span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every method→basis pair
% below was read out of a fetched Chemistry LibreTexts page, and any pair whose
% basis could not be verified there would have been dropped). Each pair, with
% the primary source it was copied from and the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   filtration → by_particle_size
%     "Filtration is a separation method used to separate out pure substances in
%      mixtures comprised of particles—some of which are large enough in size to
%      be captured with a porous material. Particle size can vary considerably,
%      given the type of mixture."
%
%   distillation → by_volatility
%     "In simple distillation, a mixture is heated, and the most volatile
%      component vaporizes at the lowest temperature."
%
%   evaporation → separates_liquid_from_solid
%     "The method drives off the liquid components from the solid components."
%
%   chromatography → by_different_rates
%     "Chromatography is the separation of a mixture by passing it in solution or
%      suspension, or as a vapor (as in gas chromatography), through a medium in
%      which the components move at different rates."
%
% All four spans are from the same Chemistry LibreTexts teaching page
% (Portland Community College, CH100 Everyday Chemistry, 1.16 Methods for
% Separating Mixtures):
% https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Portland_Community_College/CH100:_Everyday_Chemistry/01:_Matter_and_Measurements/1.16:_Methods_for_Separating_Mixtures
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the LibreTexts statement that
% fixes the first row (filtration → by_particle_size). Every OTHER row's
% verbatim span is listed above, so each basis remains independently checkable.
% Chemistry LibreTexts is a curated open-education teaching resource (a teaching
% summary, not a primary standards body), so `trust consensus`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table separation_basis {
    columns method, basis

    row (filtration, by_particle_size)
    row (distillation, by_volatility)
    row (evaporation, separates_liquid_from_solid)
    row (chromatography, by_different_rates)

    source "Filtration is a separation method used to separate out pure substances in mixtures comprised of particles—some of which are large enough in size to be captured with a porous material. Particle size can vary considerably, given the type of mixture."
    locator "https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Portland_Community_College/CH100:_Everyday_Chemistry/01:_Matter_and_Measurements/1.16:_Methods_for_Separating_Mixtures"
    trust consensus
}
